          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is it hard to keep taking a preventer inhaler every day when you feel fine?",
          "answer": "The retrieved material touches on this only partly. It does not directly explain why feeling well makes daily use hard.\n\nTwo reasons it does mention:\n\n- **Side effects.** Inhaled steroid controllers can cause hoarseness and mouth thrush. The asthma adherence summary says these minor problems are among the reasons people quietly stop. Rinsing the mouth and spitting after each dose reduces them. (Source: *Sticking with asthma treatment: inhaler technique, controllers and action plans*.)\n- **Confusing controllers with relievers.** The same summary calls confusion between a daily inhaler and a quick-relief inhaler \"the most consequential misunderstanding in asthma.\" The text it returned cuts off before explaining why, so I can't tell you more from it.\n\nThe guidance also says that poor inhaler technique is a common, unnoticed cause of treatment not working. A clinician can check your technique, and a demonstration is more useful than a leaflet. (Source: *Sticking with asthma treatment*.)\n\nThe asthma guideline summary (*Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach*) says treatment is reviewed rather than fixed. Doses are stepped down only after three or more months of good control, and clinicians check technique and adherence before stepping up.\n\nIf feeling fine makes you want to skip doses, bring that up with the prescriber or asthma nurse. Don't change how you take it on your own. They can explain what your regular inhaler does when you have no symptoms and help you plan around the side effects.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
